{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "הזקן", :translit "ha-zakan", :gloss "the beard"} {:he "הראש או הזקן", :translit "ha-rosh o ha-zakan", :gloss "the head or the beard"} {:he "הזקן זקן אהרן", :translit "ha-zakan zakan aharon", :gloss "the beard, the beard of Aaron"} {:he "וגם את הזקן תספה במפלת סנחריב", :translit "ve-gam et ha-zakan tespeh be-miflat sancheriv", :gloss "and also the beard you will count in the downfall of Sennacherib"} {:he "בלוג שמן", :translit "be-log shemen", :gloss "with a log of oil"} {:he "שמן המשחה", :translit "shemen ha-mishchah", :gloss "anointing oil"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["הראש" "הזקן"]} {:kind :other, :note "Phrase 'וגם את הזקן' from Isaiah 7:20 read as allusion to Sennacherib's army stricken with tzara'at"} {:kind :other, :note "Reward principle: Aaron examines negaim and applies log of oil, so he merits anointing oil on his beard (Psalm 133:2)"}], :frame {:text "Isaiah 7:20 describes God using the king of Assyria like a hired razor over head, feet, and beard (וגם את הזקן). Midrashic sources identify this with Sennacherib's campaign against Jerusalem. Sanhedrin 95b records that Sennacherib's army was miraculously destroyed. The association of that downfall with tzara'at appears in midrashic readings of this verse. Aaron's role as examiner of nega'im is established at Leviticus 13:1–3; the purification procedure with a log of oil (לוג שמן) is specified at Leviticus 14:10–12. Psalm 133:2 describes the anointing oil descending from Aaron's head onto his beard.", :sources ["Isaiah 7:20" "Sanhedrin 95b" "Leviticus 13:1–3" "Leviticus 14:10–12" "Psalm 133:2"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["הראש" "הזקן"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "הראש", :refs ["Leviticus 1:8" "Leviticus 8:20" "Leviticus 9:13" "Leviticus 13:30" "1 Samuel 13:17" "2 Samuel 3:8" "2 Samuel 12:4" "2 Samuel 15:32"], :n 39, :lens :exact} {:phrase "הזקן", :refs ["Genesis 43:27" "Leviticus 13:30" "Jud